INT. LOCK-KEEPER'S COTTAGE - NIGHT

A low stone room above a canal lock. Wet rope on hooks. A brass wall telephone. A kettle on a coal stove. Water thuds against the lock gate outside.

AUGUSTINE BELLWEATHER (60s, tall, rain-soaked inspector's coat, magnifier on a cord) stands over a desk covered with tide charts.

PRU (20s, lock-keeper's apprentice, mud on her boots) sits on the stool, hugging a lantern.

AUGUSTINE
Say it again, from the top.

PRU
I heard a splash around midnight. I went down. The gate was open and the barge was gone.

AUGUSTINE
And the body?

PRU
There was no body.

AUGUSTINE
Then why does the captain say there was one?

The phone rings. Both startle. Augustine lifts the receiver.

AUGUSTINE (CONT'D)
Bellweather.

HALVORSEN (V.O.)
(filtered, tense)
Inspector. It's Halvorsen at the harbor. We've found the barge.

AUGUSTINE
Where?

HALVORSEN (V.O.)
Two locks downstream. Empty. Except for a woman's boot.

Pru goes pale. She drops the lantern. It rolls, flickers, and stays alight.

AUGUSTINE
Whose boot?

HALVORSEN (V.O.)
Size six. Leather. Mud on the heel.

Augustine lowers the phone. Looks at the girl. At her feet.

AUGUSTINE
Miss Pru. Please take off your boots.

PRU
Sir, I can explain.

AUGUSTINE
I don't doubt it.

She bends. Unlaces one boot. Her hands tremble. Then she laughs, suddenly, and pulls out a second pair from under the stool.

PRU
I swapped them. They're my mother's. I wanted to see how it felt to wear them.

Augustine stares. The kettle whistles.

HALVORSEN (V.O.)
Inspector? Are you there?

AUGUSTINE
(into phone)
Yes. I think we've been chasing a cobbler's mistake.

He hangs up. Pours the tea.
